From ee78356dfc92106cf6951ad91b359106e0ef21f0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 03:04:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] DeepSeek-V4.1: GLP steering per request, under a second
 served model name

GLP steering was a property of the engine: WEIGHTLESS_STEER_PATH steered
every request, and each covered layer folded every row explicitly (decode
grid +2.4%, prefill -1.9% in spark-ds41f's next-release qualification).
It is now a property of rows. With WEIGHTLESS_STEER_MODEL set, only the
requests made to that served model name are steered, so one engine serves
the model and its steered variant side by side; unset, every request is,
as before.

Each covered layer runs one TileLang launch over the step's rows. A block
whose row is not marked returns after loading its mark, so unmarked rows
keep their bits whatever else the step carries. A marked row's write (the
ranks' unreduced parts added in rank order, as rank_order_sum does) is
folded as mHC's post kernel folds it, every stream projected in the
reduction order of the projection this replaces, and the result left as
the row's residual with comb the identity and post zero: the next layer's
fused post/pre, an Engram layer's post, the drafter's auxiliary states and
the final collapse all fold it back exactly (a negative zero may come back
positive). The model loop's forward is the unsteered one again; it only
calls the steering and keeps the marks aligned with the rows the layers
hold (this rank's under sequence parallelism, the decoder's under CED).

The marks are an int32 buffer of the steering module, refilled in place
before every step by the model state from each request's mark (a UVA
array the host writes on add_request) and the step's query bounds on the
GPU, which adaptive verification sets; captured graphs read the replayed
step's marks.

The OpenAI server marks a request to the steered name in its sampling
parameters' extra_args (clearing a caller's mark), and its response
reports the name asked for. Chat completions (and messages), completions,
responses and tokenization serve the name; every other server, and beam
search, refuses it rather than serving it unsteered. The scheduler keys a
marked request's first prefix block apart, as a cache salt does, since
steered rows write other KV.

Tests: tests/models/test_deepseek_v4_1_glp.py (the module, its marks and
dispatch), tests/test_weightless.py (marks, names, refusals, cache keys),
tests/kernels/test_deepseek_v41_tilelang_glp.py (on SM12x: a marked row
within one rounding of the FP64 projection of mHC's fold, folded back
exactly at world sizes 1-4, unmarked rows bit for bit untouched, batch
independence, and the row marks).

